    "lawCode": "Amd §§198, 215 & 663, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A6963\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn  act  to amend the labor law, in relation to strengthening wage and\nhour protections for working people\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis  bill  would  expand  worker  protections  and  remedies  against\nemployers  who  violate Labor Law requirements related to wage payment\nor who retaliate against employees who exercise their rights under the\nLabor Law.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Labor Law § 198(1-a)  to  allow  the\nCommissioner  of  Labor  to  bring  a  court  action or administrative\nproceeding to collect wage underpayments  and  liquidated  damages  on\nbehalf  of  workers, unless the employer proves a good faith basis for\nbelieving its underpayment complied with the law.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend Labor Law §  215(1)  to  expand  the\ncategories  of  conduct  protected  against  employer  retaliation for\ncomplaints concerning wage and  other  labor  law  violations,  extend\nliability  for  such  violations  to  limited  liability companies and\npartnerships, increase the minimum civil penalty from $200  to  $1,000\nand  the  maximum  penalty  from $2,000 to $10,000 for retaliation and\nallow the Commissioner to award lost  compensation  to  employees  who\nhave been victims of employer retaliation.\n\nSection  3  of  the  bill  would  amend  Labor Law § 663(1) and (2) to\nclarify that the Commissioner may  use  an  administrative  proceeding\nagainst employers to collect minimum wage underpayments and liquidated\ndamages,  and  to provide that such liquidated damages may be assessed\nunless the employer proves a good faith basis for believing  its  wage\nunderpayment complied with the law.\n\nSection  4  of  the  bill  establishes an effective date 90 days after\nenactment.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nLabor Law § 198(1-a) places the burden of proof on employees  to  show\nentitlement  to  liquidated damages by showing that the employer acted\nwillfully.\n\nLabor Law §  215(1)  establishes  the  grounds  to  claim  retaliation\nagainst   workers   who   assert   certain  labor  rights  and  allows\nCommissioner to impose civil penalties against employers.\n\nLabor Law §663 allows an employee to bring a civil action  to  recover\nminimum   wages,  and  allows  liquidated  damages  upon  proving  the\nunderpayment was willful. The same section allows the Commissioner  to\nbring  any  legal  action  necessary, including administrative action,\nagainst employers to do the same.\n\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nIn  2008,  a similar bill was introduced (A.10852/S.8208) and referred\nto the Senate Rules Committee.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nIt is not uncommon for employees to lose their jobs or have their work\nhours reduced after their employer  learns  they  have  requested  the\nDepartment  of  Labor's help to recover unpaid wages, or have asserted\nother rights under the Labor Law. This retaliation can also  take  the\nform  of  less  favorable  shifts,  loss  of benefits, reassignment of\nduties, and other changes to the terms and conditions  of  employment.\nRetaliation  against  employees  who  attempt  to recover unpaid wages\noften victimizes individuals who are among  the  lowest  paid  in  our\nstate. The current minimum penalty of $200 for employer retaliation is\nmore than a half century old and is woefully inadequate to provide any\ndisincentive   for   such  action.  Without  a  sufficient  deterrent,\nemployers can decide that it is easier to wait  until  the  Department\nbrings  an  enforcement  action  if their penalty will only be a small\nfine. In the past 50 years, the consumer  price  index  has  increased\nsixfold. If the $200 penalty had kept pace, it would have increased to\n$1,200  by  now.    This proposal would raise the minimum penalty to a\nmore modest amount of $1,000.\n\nThe bill would also extend liability for retaliation to  officers  and\nagents  of limited liability companies and partnerships. The number of\nLLC's in the  state  has  quadrupled  in  the  past  ten  years.  This\nincreasingly  popular  form  of  business  entity  allows employers to\noperate for profit and should be subject  to  the  same  liability  as\nother  employers who retaliate against workers. Further, this proposal\nwill protect employers throughout the State who comply with  the  wage\nlaw  by  ensuring  that  violators  do  not have an unfair competitive\nadvantage by underpaying workers.\n\nThis proposal would also conform  New  York  law  to  the  Fair  Labor\nStandards  Act  and  the  laws  of  other  states  that  allow workers\nliquidated damages once they establish that  their  employer  violated\nthe  wage law.   These jurisdictions recognize the inherent unfairness\nof requiring employees who have already proven they were underpaid  to\nfurther  prove  the underpayment was willful. The burden of explaining\nthe basis of violations rightly rests with the employer,  rather  than\nimposing  the  onerous  burden  on  employees  of  establishing  their\nemployer's state of mind. Many of the  employees  that  would  benefit\nfrom  this  proposal  are low-wage workers struggling to support their\nfamilies on the minimum wage. In many cases, employers have failed for\nyears to pay even the well-publicized minimum wage rate  of  $7.15  an\nhour.  The  bill  would  not  impact the 25% cap on liquidated damages\nallowed to New York employers, unlike many  other  jurisdictions  that\nallow recovery of 100% liquidated damages.\n\nCurrent  law  also limits the Commissioner's remedies against employer\nretaliation. Such retaliation harms not only the employee involved, it\nalso interferes with the Commissioner's overall  enforcement  of  wage\nand  hour  laws  by  chilling the willingness of other workers to file\ncomplaints, testify, or cooperate with investigations. This bill would\nprovide the Commissioner with broader  powers  to  punish  retaliatory\n\n\naction  through  the  recovery  of  lost compensation for a discharged\nemployee.\n\nStrengthening  the  recovery  of unpaid wages will also help the state\nand local economies. Wage complaints prompt investigations and lead to\norders against employers to recover unpaid  wages  which  are  taxable\nincome.  Each  dollar  earned  and  recovered  reduces  the  amount of\nassistance the state may have to provide to workers to help heat their\nhomes and feed their  families.  During  2006-2007,  the  Commissioner\ncollected  $15.3  million  in  minimum wage underpayments on behalf of\nmore than 19,000 workers, nearly  half  of  whom  live  upstate.  Each\ndollar  recovered  also  returns  between $1.50 - $2.00 to the economy\nthrough household purchases, bill payments,  and  other  contributions\nthat strengthen the state and local tax bases.\n\nUnfortunately,  this  increased wage recovery effort has also resulted\nin more  cases  of  wage  retaliation.  For  example,  the  Department\nrecently  helped 22 workers at a company recover approximately $10,000\nin unpaid wages. Three of these  workers  were  terminated  after  the\nemployer  learned  of their wage complaints. If this proposal had been\nin effect, the Department could have recovered as much as  $30,000  in\ncivil  penalties,  rather  than the maximum amount of $6,000 available\nunder current law.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe penalty increases are  estimated  to  generate  $75,000  in  state\nrevenues.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe  bill  would  take effect ninety days after enactment and apply to\noffenses committed on or after the effective date.\n\n",
